{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/langgraph-intro?scriptVersionId=370124744\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Why Does LangGraph Exist?\n\n## 1. Understanding Workflows and Agents\n\nBefore understanding why LangGraph exists, we need to understand the difference between workflows and agents.\n\n### Workflows\n\nWorkflows are systems in which **LLMs and tools are orchestrated through predefined code paths.**\n\n- The steps are already defined.\n- The execution follows a predefined structure.\n- The developer has control over the flow of execution.\n\n### Agents\n\nAgents are systems in which **LLMs dynamically direct their own processes and tool usage while maintaining control over how they accomplish a task.**\n\n- The LLM decides what action to take.\n- It can dynamically decide which tool to use.\n- It can decide what to do next based on the current situation.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Understanding the Automated Hiring Workflow\n\nLet's take an example of an automated hiring workflow.\n\nSuppose a company wants to automate its entire hiring process using AI.\n\nThe workflow looks something like this:\n\n### Step 1: Start and Hiring Request\n\n- The workflow starts when a company raises a hiring request.\n- The company provides details about the position it wants to hire for.\n\n### Step 2: Create Job Description (JD)\n\n- An LLM is used to generate a Job Description (JD) based on the hiring requirements.\n- The generated JD is sent for approval.\n\n### Step 3: JD Approval\n\nWe check whether the JD is approved or not.\n\n- **If No:** The workflow goes back to the Create JD step, and the JD is modified or generated again.\n- **If Yes:** The workflow moves forward.\n\n### Step 4: Post JD\n\n- The approved JD is posted on a job platform such as LinkedIn.\n- This can be implemented using tools, such as the LinkedIn API.\n\n### Step 5: Wait for 7 Days\n\n- After posting the JD, the workflow waits for 7 days to collect applications.\n\n### Step 6: Monitor Applications\n\n- The workflow monitors the applications received.\n- It checks whether enough applications have been received.\n\n### Step 7: Enough Applications?\n\nWe check whether the required number of applications has been received.\n\n- **If No:** Modify the JD, wait for 48 hours, and then monitor the applications again.\n- **If Yes:** Move forward to the shortlisting process.\n\n### Step 8: Shortlist Candidates\n\n- The system shortlists suitable candidates from the received applications.\n\n### Step 9: Schedule Interviews\n\n- Interviews are scheduled for shortlisted candidates.\n- Tools such as Calendar and Mail can be used to schedule interviews and send emails.\n- The workflow can also involve people responsible for conducting interviews (for example, Nikhil and Rahul).\n\n### Step 10: Conduct Interview\n\n- The shortlisted candidates appear for their interviews.\n\n### Step 11: Candidate Selected?\n\nWe check whether the candidate is selected or not.\n\n- **If No:** Send a regret email to the candidate.\n- **If Yes:** Move forward to the offer letter process.\n\n### Step 12: Send Offer Letter\n\n- An offer letter is sent to the selected candidate.\n- This step can also involve a tool to send the offer letter.\n\n### Step 13: Offer Accepted?\n\nWe check whether the candidate has accepted the offer.\n\n- **If Yes:** Move to the onboarding process.\n- **If No:** Renegotiate the offer and send the offer letter again.\n\n### Step 14: Onboarding and End\n\n- Once the candidate accepts the offer, the onboarding process starts.\n- After successful onboarding, the workflow ends.\n\n**Important:** This entire workflow contains conditional branches, loops, backward jumps, waiting periods, external triggers, and human involvement.\n\n---\n\n# 3. What If We Want to Implement This Workflow Using LangChain?\n\nIf we try to implement the entire automated hiring workflow using LangChain, we will face multiple challenges.\n\n## Challenge 1: Control Flow Complexity\n\nWe know that LangChain chains are generally used to build linear workflows.\n\nHowever, our automated hiring workflow is completely non-linear.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If the JD is not approved, we go back to the Create JD step.\n- If enough applications are not received, we modify the JD and wait for another 48 hours.\n- If the candidate is not selected, we send a regret email.\n- If the candidate does not accept the offer, we renegotiate and send the offer letter again.\n\nThis means our workflow contains:\n\n- Conditional branching\n- Loops\n- Backward and forward jumps\n- Multiple execution paths\n\nThere is no built-in graph-based workflow orchestration in basic LangChain chains to manage this kind of complex flow directly.\n\nTo implement these loops and conditional paths, we would have to write additional Python code.\n\nThis additional code is called **Glue Code**.\n\n**What is Glue Code?**\n\nGlue code is the code we write to connect different components or pieces of code together so that they work as a complete system.\n\nHowever, writing too much glue code creates problems:\n\n- Code becomes difficult to maintain.\n- The workflow becomes complicated.\n- Debugging becomes difficult.\n- Managing multiple execution paths becomes hectic.\n\n**How does LangGraph solve this?**\n\nIn LangGraph, we represent our complete workflow in the form of a graph.\n\n- Every step of the workflow is represented as a node.\n- The connections between nodes are represented as edges.\n- Conditional edges decide which path the workflow should follow.\n\nTherefore, we can easily represent non-linear workflows using LangGraph.\n\nFor example, in our hiring workflow, the JD approval decision can either send the workflow back to Create JD or move it forward to Post JD.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 2: Handling State\n\nIn our automated hiring workflow, multiple data points are important throughout the process.\n\nFor example:\n\n- What is the generated Job Description (JD)?\n- Has the JD been approved?\n- Where has the JD been posted?\n- How many people have applied till date?\n- What is the minimum number of applications required to start the interview process?\n- How many candidates have been shortlisted?\n- What are the shortlisted candidates' contact details?\n- To how many candidates have we sent offer letters?\n- What is the current status of each offer?\n- What is the onboarding status of the selected candidate?\n\nAll these data points keep changing throughout the workflow.\n\nThe collection of these changing data points is called the **State of the Workflow**.\n\nIf we don't track the state properly, our workflow will not work correctly.\n\n### How is state represented?\n\nThe state of a workflow can be represented using key-value pairs.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\nstate = {\n    \"job_description\": \"Backend Developer\",\n    \"jd_approved\": True,\n    \"applications_received\": 20,\n    \"minimum_applications\": 10,\n    \"shortlisted_candidates\": [],\n    \"offer_status\": \"pending\",\n    \"onboarding_status\": \"not_started\"\n}\n```\n\n### The problem with LangChain\n\nLangChain has the concept of Memory, but its traditional memory components are primarily designed for conversational memory.\n\nThey can store information related to conversations with LLMs.\n\nHowever, managing the complete state of a large workflow requires more than conversational memory.\n\nIf we want to implement and track all these workflow data points ourselves, we have to manage them manually.\n\nThis becomes very hectic when we are working with large and complex workflows.\n\n### How does LangGraph solve this?\n\nWhen we create a graph in LangGraph, we also define a State object (generally represented as a dictionary or a typed state schema).\n\n- Every node of the graph can access this state.\n- Every node can read the required information.\n- Every node can update the state.\n- The updated state is passed through the workflow as it executes.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The Create JD node updates the JD in the state.\n- The JD Approval node updates the approval status.\n- The Monitor Applications node updates the application count.\n- The Shortlist node updates the shortlisted candidates.\n\nTherefore, LangGraph provides a structured way to manage and track the state of a workflow.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 3: Event-Driven Execution\n\nWhenever we create a workflow, it can execute in different ways.\n\nTwo important execution patterns are:\n\n### 1. Sequential Execution\n\nA sequential workflow executes its steps one after another without waiting for an external event or trigger between steps.\n\n### 2. Event-Driven Execution\n\nIn event-driven execution, the workflow can pause and wait for an external event or trigger before moving forward.\n\nLet's understand this using our automated hiring workflow.\n\nThere are multiple places where we cannot immediately move to the next step.\n\nFor example:\n\n- After posting the JD, we have to wait for 7 days.\n- After modifying the JD, we have to wait for 48 hours.\n- After sending an offer letter, we have to wait for the candidate's response.\n- Before proceeding with the hiring process, we may need human approval.\n\nThe workflow needs to pause and resume when the required time has passed or an external event occurs.\n\n### The problem with LangChain\n\nLangChain's basic chain-based execution is primarily designed for sequential workflows.\n\nIt does not provide the same built-in orchestration capabilities for long-running, event-driven workflows.\n\nAgain, we would have to write a lot of glue code to manage these pauses, triggers, and resumptions.\n\n### How does LangGraph solve this?\n\nLangGraph supports event-driven and long-running workflow execution.\n\nIt allows us to:\n\n- Pause the workflow.\n- Wait for an external trigger.\n- Resume execution from where it was paused.\n\nThis makes it suitable for workflows that cannot be completed in a single continuous execution.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 4: Fault Tolerance\n\n**Fault tolerance** means the ability of a system to handle failures and recover from them so that the system can continue working.\n\nLet's understand this using our hiring workflow.\n\nSuppose our workflow has completed these steps:\n\n1. Generated the JD.\n2. Got the JD approved.\n3. Posted the JD on LinkedIn.\n4. Collected applications.\n5. Shortlisted candidates.\n\nNow, suppose an error occurs while scheduling interviews.\n\nWe should not have to execute the entire workflow again from the beginning.\n\nWe should be able to recover from the failure and continue the workflow.\n\n### The problem with LangChain\n\nIn a basic chain-based implementation, handling failures across a long-running workflow requires us to implement recovery mechanisms ourselves.\n\nIf we don't implement them properly, we may have to execute the entire chain again.\n\n### How does LangGraph solve this?\n\nLangGraph provides capabilities for handling failures and recovering from them.\n\nIt supports different ways of handling failures:\n\n**1. Retry**\n\n- Used for small or temporary failures.\n- The failed operation can be attempted again.\n\nExample: If sending an email fails due to a temporary network issue, we can retry the operation.\n\n**2. Recovery**\n\n- Used when a bigger failure occurs.\n- The workflow can recover from its saved state and continue execution instead of starting everything again.\n\nExample: If an error occurs during interview scheduling, the workflow can recover and continue from the appropriate point.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 5: Human-in-the-Loop\n\nHuman-in-the-loop means involving a human in the workflow when human approval or intervention is required.\n\nLet's understand this using our hiring workflow.\n\nSuppose we generate a Job Description using an LLM.\n\nBefore posting it on LinkedIn, the company wants a human to review and approve the JD.\n\nUntil the approval comes, the workflow should not continue.\n\nTherefore:\n\n1. The LLM generates the JD.\n2. The workflow pauses.\n3. A human reviews the JD.\n4. The human approves or rejects it.\n5. If approved, the workflow continues.\n6. If rejected, the workflow goes back to modify the JD.\n\nThis process of pausing a workflow and waiting for human input is called **Human-in-the-Loop (HITL)**.\n\n### The problem with LangChain\n\nLangChain's basic chains are not designed to manage long-running workflows that need human intervention and approval.\n\nWe would have to write additional glue code to implement this functionality.\n\n### How does LangGraph solve this?\n\nLangGraph provides built-in capabilities for human-in-the-loop workflows.\n\nIt allows us to:\n\n- Pause execution.\n- Wait for human approval or input.\n- Resume execution after receiving the required input.\n\nThis is particularly useful when human decisions are important for moving forward.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 6: Nested Workflows\n\nSometimes, we may want to implement one workflow inside another workflow.\n\nThis concept is called **Nested Workflows**.\n\nLet's understand this using our automated hiring workflow.\n\nSuppose we have a separate workflow for candidate shortlisting.\n\nThis shortlisting workflow might contain:\n\n- Resume parsing.\n- Skill extraction.\n- Candidate evaluation.\n- Candidate ranking.\n- Final shortlisting.\n\nInstead of writing all these steps directly inside the main hiring workflow, we can create a separate workflow for shortlisting.\n\nThen we can connect this workflow to the main hiring workflow.\n\n### How does LangGraph solve this?\n\nLangGraph provides the concept of **Subgraphs**.\n\nA subgraph allows us to define a separate graph and use it as a part of another graph.\n\n- The main graph represents the complete hiring workflow.\n- A subgraph can represent the candidate shortlisting workflow.\n- The main graph can invoke the subgraph whenever required.\n\nThis makes large workflows modular, reusable, and easier to manage.\n\n---\n\n## Challenge 7: Observability\n\n**Observability** refers to how easily we can monitor, debug, and understand what our workflow is doing at runtime.\n\nLet's understand this using our automated hiring workflow.\n\nSuppose our workflow is stuck at the interview scheduling step.\n\nWe need to understand:\n\n- Which step has been executed?\n- Which step is currently running?\n- What was the input given to that step?\n- What was the output?\n- Where did the error occur?\n- Why did the workflow stop?\n\nWithout proper observability, debugging such a large workflow becomes difficult.\n\n### The problem with LangChain and LangSmith\n\nLangChain and LangSmith can be easily integrated.\n\nHowever, if we implement complex workflow logic using additional Python glue code, LangSmith's LangChain tracing will not automatically cover all that custom code.\n\nWe may lose visibility into the parts of the workflow that are implemented outside the traced LangChain components.\n\n### How does LangGraph help?\n\nLangGraph provides graph-based execution and state tracking, which makes it easier to understand the execution of a workflow.\n\nIt also integrates with LangSmith for tracing, monitoring, and debugging graph execution.\n\nThis helps us understand what is happening at each step of the workflow.\n\n---\n\n# 4. What Is LangGraph?\n\nLangGraph is an orchestration framework that enables us to build **stateful, multi-step, and event-driven workflows** using Large Language Models (LLMs).\n\nIt is ideal for designing both single-agent and multi-agent AI applications.\n\nThink of LangGraph as a **flowchart engine for LLMs**.\n\n- We define the steps (nodes).\n- We define how the steps are connected (edges).\n- We define the logic that governs the transitions.\n\nLangGraph takes care of:\n\n- State management.\n- Conditional branching.\n- Looping.\n- Pausing and resuming.\n- Fault recovery.\n\nThese features are essential for building robust, production-grade AI systems.\n\n---\n\n# 5. When Should We Use LangChain and LangGraph?\n\n### Use LangChain when:\n\nWe are building simple, linear workflows, such as:\n\n- A prompt chain.\n- A summarizer.\n- A basic retrieval system.\n\n### Use LangGraph when:\n\nOur use case involves complex, non-linear workflows that require:\n\n- Conditional paths.\n- Loops.\n- Human-in-the-loop steps.\n- Multi-agent coordination.\n- Asynchronous or event-driven execution.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Should We Still Use LangChain?\n\n**Yes! LangGraph is built on top of LangChain. It does not replace LangChain.**\n\nWe will still use LangChain components inside our LangGraph workflows.\n\nSome important LangChain components are:\n\n1. **ChatOpenAI** – Used to interact with Large Language Models (LLMs).\n2. **PromptTemplate** – Used to create structured and reusable prompts.\n3. **Retrievers** – Used to retrieve relevant information from a knowledge source.\n4. **DocumentLoaders** – Used to load documents from different sources.\n5. **Tools** – Used to connect LLMs with external functionalities and services.\n\n### Final Understanding\n\nLangGraph handles **workflow orchestration**, while LangChain provides the **building blocks** required for individual steps in that workflow.\n\nIn simple words:\n\n- LangChain helps us build the individual components.\n- LangGraph helps us connect and orchestrate those components into a complete, complex workflow.","metadata":{}}]}